+# Decision: Self-hosted WebSocket delivery for committed changes
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard commits every edit as one authoritative event revision and must show committed changes in connected browsers within 15 seconds, recover the latest board after a browser has been disconnected for up to 20 minutes, support 800 simultaneous browsers with opening-time bursts of 20 edits per second, and retain seven days of event revisions — all at 99.5 percent availability during published event hours.
+
+The delivery mechanism sits between the change store and connected browsers, and it shapes three design nodes at once: what the store must persist (replayable revisions, reliable publication), what delivery owns (sessions, connection capacity), and how browsers subscribe, reconnect, and catch up. Three viable mechanisms exist in the domain — periodic HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, and a hosted realtime service — and the choice is load-bearing enough that a later reader may be tempted to reverse it toward the hosted option to shed connection operations. This doc records why the tradeoff was accepted.
+
+All three options can meet the 15-second freshness bound and the 20-minute recovery goal given the right supporting design, so neither acts as an eliminator; what differs is everything past those floors. Event updates carry only stall IDs and operational statuses, so every option respects the data boundary the tenets set — that too is background, not a differentiator.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+- **Update latency past the freshness floor (major).** The 15-second bound is the requirement; the point of the mechanism choice is how close to commit-time a connected browser reflects a change. Market activity clusters around opening time, where seconds of lag is the difference between a coordinator seeing a stall closure and acting on a stale board.
+- **Provider coupling of the core delivery path (major).** A hosted service makes board availability during event hours depend on a third party's SDK, credentials, quota model, and outage behavior. The delivery path is the product's spine; coupling it is a strategic commitment, not a convenience.
+- **Recurring cost (major).** Hosted realtime services bill on connections and message volume — exactly the quantities MarketBoard maxes out at (800 browsers, 20 edits/second bursts), recurring every month. Self-run mechanisms cost only the infrastructure the system already needs.
+- **Owned operational burden (major).** Whoever runs delivery owns its failure modes. Self-hosted sockets mean owning connection capacity planning, reconnect behavior at opening time, and socket-process supervision. This criterion cuts *against* the selected option and is weighed honestly, not minimized.
+- **Failure transparency (moderate).** The tenet *connectivity failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board* falls differently on each mechanism: a closed socket is an explicit signal, while a stalled poll loop is silent.
+- **Reuse of the required revision history (moderate).** Seven-day revision retention is required for investigation regardless of delivery. A mechanism whose catch-up path reuses that history avoids building parallel machinery.
+- **Implementation surface across the three nodes (moderate).** Each option distributes work differently across the store, delivery, and browser-sync nodes; the total matters less than where it lands and how much of it is needed anyway.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+**Description.** Browsers poll a board snapshot or delta endpoint on a fixed five-second loop over ordinary request/response. The store serves cursor queries ("everything since revision N"); delivery stays stateless request/response; browsers run the poll loop and render deltas. Simplest possible operations — nothing holds state, nothing connects, HTTP infrastructure already required for commands serves reads too.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Update latency: **weak** — a change waits out the remainder of the poll interval plus request overhead, so typical lag is several seconds and worst-case sits at the interval's edge.
+- Provider coupling: **strong** — no third party in the path.
+- Recurring cost: **strong** — no new cost; reads ride existing infrastructure.
+- Owned operational burden: **strong** — stateless; no connections to plan or supervise.
+- Failure transparency: **weak** — the tenet's named hazard: an unreachable board and an unchanged board produce the same silence, so the browser needs extra machinery to distinguish "no news" from "no connection."
+- Reuse of revision history: **partial** — cursor queries reuse the required revisions, but each browser re-reads on every cycle whether or not anything changed.
+- Implementation surface: **partial** — the store gains cursor query indexes and the browser gains a poll loop; the repeated-read traffic scales with browsers × interval, worst precisely at opening time.
+
+**Summary.** Buys stateless simplicity at the price of lag and read amplification.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+**Description.** The server runs a WebSocket endpoint browsers connect to for an event. The change store persists replayable revisions and publishes each committed revision reliably; delivery manages sessions and pushes published revisions to subscribed connections; browsers hold their last-seen revision, reconnect with backoff, and replay missed revisions to catch up. Delivery is an owned server process with capacity sized for 800 simultaneous browsers.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Update latency: **strong** — a committed revision reaches subscribed browsers at publish time; connected browsers update essentially on commit.
+- Provider coupling: **strong** — the path is entirely owned.
+- Recurring cost: **strong** — infrastructure cost only.
+- Owned operational burden: **weak** — the system owns connection capacity, reconnect storms at opening time, and socket supervision. Accepted deliberately (see Selection).
+- Failure transparency: **strong** — a closed or dropped socket is an explicit client-visible signal, directly serving the connectivity-failure tenet.
+- Reuse of revision history: **strong** — reconnect catch-up replays the same revisions the store must retain anyway; no parallel mechanism.
+- Implementation surface: **partial** — the store gains reliable publication, delivery gains session management, browsers gain reconnect/catch-up — real work, but each piece is machinery the other two options need in some form too (cursors, reconciliation, or vendor glue), while polling and hosted each still leave the 20-minute recovery problem to this same replay path.
+
+**Summary.** Fast, uncoupled, and transparent, at the price of owning connection operations.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+**Description.** Delivery wraps a vendor realtime platform: the server publishes committed revisions to vendor channels, browsers subscribe through the vendor's client SDK. The store needs publication reconciliation (verifying what the vendor actually delivered against what was committed); delivery manages provider credentials, quotas, and channel mapping; connection capacity and reconnect handling are the vendor's product.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- Update latency: **strong** — vendor push networks deliver at commit-comparable latency.
+- Provider coupling: **weak** — the board's core delivery path depends on one vendor's SDK, credential lifecycle, quota model, and outages; the 99.5 percent availability goal becomes partly outside the system's control.
+- Recurring cost: **weak** — fees scale with simultaneous connections and message volume, recurring monthly, and are largest exactly at MarketBoard's peak (800 browsers, opening bursts).
+- Owned operational burden: **partial** — connection operations shift to the vendor, but outages and limits become vendor-support work rather than system work.
+- Failure transparency: **partial** — vendor SDKs expose connection state, but failure modes and their visibility are the vendor's design, not the tenet's own.
+- Reuse of revision history: **partial** — catch-up can use replay, but the vendor's delivery state is a second source of truth that must be reconciled with the store's revisions.
+- Implementation surface: **partial** — publication reconciliation and provider integration are new machinery unique to this option.
+
+**Summary.** Buys low-latency push with outsourced connection ops at the price of fees and core-path coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Self-hosted WebSockets.** It is the only option that is strong on all three cost-and-control majors — latency past the freshness floor, no provider coupling, no recurring fees — and it is the strongest fit to the connectivity-transparency tenet: a dropped socket is a visible event, where polling's silence is indistinguishable from an unchanged board. The choice leans on the tenet that self-hosted and managed infrastructure are both *permitted* — it takes the self-hosted side within that permission rather than being forced by it.
+
+The tradeoff is real and was accepted with eyes open: this option is **weak on owned operational burden**, and that weakness is the price of the rest. Connection capacity for 800 simultaneous browsers, reconnect behavior under opening-time bursts, and socket-process supervision are now the system's to run during published event hours — accepted in exchange for fast updates without tying the board to a realtime vendor.
+
+The one-authoritative-revision tenet is untouched by the choice: delivery transmits revisions the store has already committed; no delivery route mints or merges revisions of its own.
+
+**What this forecloses:** subsecond delivery without owning connection operations is off the table until a future decision revisits the hosted option. **What this obligates downstream:** the change store must persist replayable revisions and publish each committed revision reliably; delivery must own session capacity and publication to subscribed connections; browser sync must own reconnection with backoff and revision catch-up (carrying the 20-minute recovery goal) and must surface disconnection visibly. If connection operations prove unsustainable during event hours, the hosted option remains the retreat path — replayable revisions and browser catch-up transfer to it, with publication reconciliation added.
